<p>A joint is where two bones meet, enclosed by a joint capsule. A joint injection places medication, usually a local anesthetic and a low-dose steroid, directly into the joint space. The goal is to reduce inflammation and swelling in the joint, which may in turn reduce pain and other symptoms caused by irritation of the joint and surrounding structures.</p>
<h3>Trigger point injections</h3>
<p>Trigger points are tender areas in a muscle that feel like knots or tight bands. When pressed, they can send pain to other parts of the body. They may be limited to one area, such as the muscles that support the neck, or appear in several areas at once.</p>
<p>A trigger point injection delivers a small amount of medication into the tender muscle. Depending on your evaluation, this may be a local anesthetic, a corticosteroid, ketorolac, saline or dextrose. Local anesthetics increase blood flow in the muscle, which helps clear the inflammatory substances that build up when a muscle spasms. Corticosteroids help block inflammation. The fluid itself also helps break up muscle spasm and adhesions.</p>
<h3>Low-dose and non-steroidal options</h3>
<p>When a steroid is part of your injection, we use the lowest effective dose. The main purpose of a low-dose injection is to numb and calm the tissues around a painful area. Combined with the other therapies we offer, this approach is designed to provide relief while limiting steroid exposure. We use it for orthopedic pain, joint inflammation and painful ligament, nerve, tendon and overuse conditions.</p>
<h3>Prescription management</h3>
<p>Some conditions call for prescription management alongside in-office therapy. The goal is the best possible comfort, function and quality of life with the fewest side effects.</p>
